Work out the next value yourself and use that variable, instead of reading state after you set it:

const next = count + 1;
setCount(next);
fetch(`/api/counts/${next}`);

That way the API call and the state update use the same number. If the update depends on the previous value in a more complicated way, you can also use the functional form, setCount(prev => prev + 1), but that only helps with the state itself. The code that runs right after still can't see the new value, so it needs the local variable.

If the API call should happen whenever the count changes, the useEffect approach is usually the cleaner choice.
